WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:05.819
We're chopping down the thicket of necessary
federal regulations

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:11.000
by which government too often interferes in
our personal lives and out personal business.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.060
When president Trump bragged about cutting
red tape during his first state of the union address-

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.500
We have undertaken a historic effort to massively
reduce job crushing regulations.

00:00:23.509 --> 00:00:27.349
-the Democratic response was hardly a surprise.

00:00:27.349 --> 00:00:31.880
But Democrats may be surprised to learn that
at the dawn of the deregulation era in the 1970s

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.820
it was their party pushing to cut red tape
and shut down entire agencies.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.600
A leading figure in the fight to slash the
state was none other than Jimmy Carter.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.460
There is a limit to the role and the function
of government.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.580
It was senator Ted Kennedy who held extensive
Senate hearings in the early 70s

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.760
with testimony from the likes of Ralph Nader
and liberal economist Alfred Kahn

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.400
about the benefits of deregulating the airline
industry.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.680
The resulting Airline Deregulation Act of
1978, signed by Carter,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.600
killed the Civil Aeronautics Board, a federal
agency that decided which airlines could fly where

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.480
and even what they could charge.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.740
The new competition to the old airline cartel
reduced fares, expanded destinations,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.980
and made air travel an option for those of
us who aren't rich.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:21.780
On Southern there's only one class of service
and it isn't second.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.180
Carter also looked at stifling government
oversight of the rail and trucking industries

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.640
under a Democrat controlled House and Senate.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.140
Result: competition intensified, prices dropped

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.200
and consumers saved more money on every day
products.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.290
We've deregulated the air industry, the rail
industry, the trucking industry, financial institutions.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:45.850
In 1978 President Carter signed into law a
bill that limited Prohibition era restrictions

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.220
on home brewing

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.640
fully legalizing do it yourself beer production
fostered a culture of experimentation

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:55.840
paving the way for a craft beer revolution
beginning in the 1980s and 90s.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.560
The year that Carter loosened the rules, the
US was home to a mere 50 breweries,

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.060
today there are over 5000.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.890
In two generations America went from global
laughing stock to world leader

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:10.080
producing the richest lagers and tastiest
ales.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.320
The Governor of California during Carter's
first Presidency was none other than Jerry Brown

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:15.740
then known as Governor Moonbeam

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:19.680
for his far out musings, glittery social life
and lefty politics.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.360
Yet Brown too could be a fiery skeptic of
government.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.189
And there is an overblown rhetoric and an
overblown expectation

00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:30.360
that if there is a problem, there must be
a program to solve it.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.840
In his legitimately awesome second inaugural
address, back in 1979,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.440
Brown said that, quote:

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:43.380
'Many regulations primarily protect the past,
prop up privilege or prevent sensible economic choices.'

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:48.319
The idea that government has some omnipotence
or omniscience

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.580
is completely absurd and counter to all of
the thinking that went into this country.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:57.530
So air travel is now competitive, though it
should be more open to foreign competition damn it!

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.440
And the beer industry has been reborn.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.620
But since the dawn of the disco era, governments
at the local, state and federal levels

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.439
have imposed entirely new categories of regulations,
such as occupational licensing,

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.440
which applied to about one in ten jobs 40
years ago, but now effects one in three.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.480
Arizona, for example, will put its' citizens
in jail for six months

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.060
for operating a blow drier without a permit.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.520
Texas wants to make it a criminal offense
to buy pecans without proper authorization.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.799
And Washington DC wants to require that all
day care providers have a college degree.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.040
The aggregate cost of these regulations is
staggering.

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.540
Last year, according to the free market Competitive
Enterprise Institute,

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:41.930
federal regulations alone cost Americans 1.9
trillion dollars

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.709
or about 15 grand per household.

00:03:44.709 --> 00:03:47.400
And while Trump has cut the number of pages
in the federal register

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.900
by about one third since he took office,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.040
There are still some 67 federal departments,
agencies and commissions

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.169
currently working on over 3000 new regulations.

00:03:58.169 --> 00:04:02.909
It seems you can't stop the regulatory state,
you can only hope to contain it.

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:05.999
As Alfred Kahn and his contemporaries could
have explained

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.460
licensing boards tend to be dominated by industry
incumbents

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:12.680
who are mainly interested in protecting their
own turf.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:17.200
Regulation is the friend, not enemy of big
business.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.420
So what has changed over the years

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:23.020
about the party of Bernie Sanders, Elizabeth
Warren and Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez?

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:28.700
Democratic support for deregulation was partly
born out of desperation in the age of stagflation.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.120
Cutting red tape to foster dynamism was about
the last move politicians had left.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.820
By acting now we can control our future instead
of letting the future control us.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:43.880
So when the long economic expansion and stock
market boom we enjoy now ends, and it will,

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.000
the best thing of law makers of both parties
to ask themselves

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.640
might just be , what would Jimmy Carter do?

